    {
      "question": "e-74",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "3599111b780b81886a35caf490125659d62f42f43ae8a018b4dc7cd951e6c5e2",
      "new_sha256": "60cf8e3f0403a342725498de4d6ce3a1b37bd8b116a9ef3af45145f2dc302bfb",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-74",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-2\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & =\\frac{4-2\\sqrt{4}}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {(x -2 \\sqrt{x})(x+2\\sqrt x)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n            &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-\\left(2\\sqrt{x}\\right)^2} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-4x} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x(x-4)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x} {x+2\\sqrt x} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {4} {4+2\\sqrt4} \\\n\t    &=\\dfrac 1 2.\n\t}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037545/example-74.json",
          "file_sha256": "849f4caa91600db7bb93204342c6a29d7cbf3630cc9db68c8cbd89cbebbbcd56"
        }
